messages:
rachel: Quick sync - noticed a parity issue between Java and Kotlin samples around cancellation + timeouts on Android 14. Java sample's Future wrapper doesn't cancel the underlying OkHttp Call, so WorkManager cancellation leaves network flapping calls alive.

evan: Oof. Can you paste the Java snippet?

rachel: Java sample: ```java
Call call = retrofitService.upload(...);
CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
  try { Response r = call.execute(); } catch (IOException e) { ... }
});
// cancel path just calls future.cancel(false)
```

That cancel(false) doesn't interrupt or call.call.cancel(), so underlying socket remains until timeout. On flaky networks that means duplicate payloads after re-network.

sara: Kotlin sample uses coroutine adapter and we wire cancellation to call.cancel() in the adapter. We should either update Java sample to call.cancel() or add a small utility wrapper that maps future cancel -> call.cancel().

rachel: Also spotted different default timeout behavior: Java sample sets readTimeout(0) for streaming; combined with retryOnConnectionFailure(true) you can end up with long-lived connections that don't respect worker lifecycle. Suggest we add an explicit note in both samples about when to use readTimeout(0) and how to handle streaming vs short requests.

evan: Another subtlety: Android 14 changed how connection reuse / keepalive is reported which can increase new connection creation in some OEM images. That surfaced as more frequent TLS handshakes and more Authorization headers observed (more requests, not reuse). We should call out to QA to validate connection reuse metrics on Pixel 8/14.

sara: Proposed fixes for samples: (1) Java sample: show proper cancel integration (call.cancel() when future cancelled) and a coroutine-based alt snippet; (2) default retryOnConnectionFailure=false for upload flows; (3) lower maxRequestsPerHost to 6 in samples; (4) add a short section in README explaining call cancellation semantics and readTimeout choices.

rachel: For Java cancellation we can do: ```java
Call call = service.upload(...);
CompletableFuture<Response> fut = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> call.execute());
// cancel listener
fut.whenComplete((r, t) -> { if (fut.isCancelled()) call.cancel(); });
```

evan: That works, but also include explicit try/catch for InterruptedException paths if we use interruptible IO. In tests we should assert that cancelling the wrapper results in call.isCanceled()==true within short window.

sara: I opened a PR draft with changes plus an integration test that toggles emulator network (shell svc data enable/disable) and asserts only one token refresh when many uploads start after offline -> online. Link: https://github.com/redwood/sampleapp/pull/5012 (WIP). It updates Java sample and improves docs.

devx-bot: heads-up: PR 5012 added sample changes (WIP). Recommended: run emulator-pixel8 + legacy-device matrix.

rachel: Nice. For the test we stub tokenProvider.refresh() and count calls; offline->online triggers a batch of 10 uploads and we assert refreshCalls <= 1 for a single expiry. This will catch regression where adapter cancellation missing causes duplicate refreshes.

evan: Also add a small helper in SDK: public util CancelableCall.wrap(Call) that returns an executor-backed Future with proper cancel semantics. Could be useful for Java users who don't use coroutines.

sara: Good idea. I'll add CancelableCall in samples/utils and point to it from Java quickstart.

ci-bot: ci: PR 5012 - tests queued: emulator-pixel8, emulator-pixel6, integration-suite.

rachel: Thanks. I'll validate PR locally on Pixel 8 and an Android 10 device and post results. If anyone sees flaky TLS session issues on Android 14 please record connection pool metrics (dispatcher.runningCallsCount, queuedCalls, connectionPool.connectionCount) and paste them in this thread.

sara: Will do. Planning to land after green CI and a small follow-up that documents readTimeout tradeoffs. :sparkles:
